  "query_preview": "..\nSunday night through Thursday: The ridge of high pressure over\nthe Inland Northwest temporarily flattens Sunday night into \nMonday as a weather system drops into the Gulf of Alaska and \nthen tracks toward British Columbia. A weak shortwave rippling\nthrough the southern periphery of this system will bring an\nincrease in mid and high level clouds as well as breezy westerly winds\nfor WA and North ID Monday afternoon and evening. Impacts with\nthis wave will be minimal with gusts topping out around 20 mph\nthrough the Cascade gaps, over the Waterville Plateau, and \nacross the Columbia Basin. Heading into Tuesday, the ridge of\nhigh pressure re-amplifies over the region, bringing a return of\nclear skies and light winds.\n\nAs for temperatures, afternoon highs will hover in the 70s to\nlow 80s, 10 to 15 degrees above climatological normals for \nearly October. Despite the warm daytime temperatures,\novernight/early morning lows will still be quite chilly. Strong\nradiational cooling under clear skies and light winds will\nsupport low temperatures dropping into the mid to upper 30s for\nthe sheltered northern valleys. Frost and patchy fog has been\nobserved in these valleys the past couple of mornings and will\nlikely recur each morning through Thursday. South of a line from\nWaterville to Spokane, lows will generally be in the 40s.\n\n\nFriday and Saturday: Confidence is high in a pattern shift\nheading into the weekend. 80 percent of ensemble members\nindicate a trough moving in from off the Pacific, though details\nremain uncertain regarding the strength and track of the trough.\nIn general, expect temperatures to trend cooler with gusty winds\nand returning precipitation chances. /Fewkes",
